Tao will answer ones that are quick; he types fast.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Ones that take a little longer to respond to or ones where we have to look for the answers,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.000
we will get back to you on that.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
We'll put those things up, and they'll link them to the Webinars page.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.000
And the URL for our new version of our Webinars page is there at the bottom of this slide.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.000
This is a link to our Learn button that's on the front of our Home Page now.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.000
This morning we're going to talk about a utility called MOLE-BLAST.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
MOLE-BLAST is a way of clustering sequences and finding their nearest neighbors in the NCBI sequence database.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
It then makes a phylogenetic tree of those sequences.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
And what's it's good for is for working with what I'm going to called targeted sequences.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.000
We often call these things targeted loci.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.000
These are things like ribosomal RNA genes from bacteria or fungi

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.000
or even things like cytochrome oxidase 1.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:05.000
It uses BLAST to do the initial clustering and generates a multiple alignment using MUSCLE and then a phylogenetic tree.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.000
People who use this could be people like taxonomists, people who are interested in evolutionary biology,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.000
people who are doing ecological studies with metagenomes.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.000
And of course we use it here to confirm identifications and to see if something might be misidentified.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Certainly people that submit to the NCBI can use this tool to identify their own sequences

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.000
and confirm that they are what they think they are.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.000
The materials and the examples that I'm going to use are linked to the FTP site, and they're in there.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.000
And I'm going to go ahead and go now to the BLAST Home Page and then link to MOLE-BLAST.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.000
So let me go ahead and do that here.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Actually, I think what I'm going to do is escape from my slide set and we'll just go there directly.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Let me go ahead and do that.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.000
What I'm going to do is go over here; and this is the NCBI Home Page, of course.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.000
And if you go to our BLAST Page, if you scroll down,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.000
MOLE-BLAST is linked down here in our Specialized BLAST section of the page.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.000
And I'm going to go ahead and follow the link to MOLE-BLAST.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:11.000
MOLE-BLAST lets you submit a list of nucleotide accessions or FASTA formatted sequences,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.000
and it gives you some guidelines as to how big those can be.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
And so we're going to go ahead and pick a set from the NCBI databases and do that real quickly.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.000
The one that I'm going to use over here that I have already loaded in one of my tabs --

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:31.000
and this is available on the FTP site -- this is a set of clones that were identified

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
or 16S ribosomal RNA genes that were sequenced from one of our PopSets.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.000
And this is from a waste water metagenome.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:46.000
And so what you can quickly do on an Entrez page like this is go ahead and set the display to Accession List,

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:57.000
hit Apply, and then I have a set of 50 accessions that I could quickly just grab, copy them,

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.000
and throw them into MOLE-BLAST.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.000
So I'm going to paste those in there, and then I can run this.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Let me just go over a couple of settings here, and we'll probably have time to do just the one example this morning.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.000
But I just want to show you how this works.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.000
I could search against the default NR database,

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.000
and that's going to be useful if I'm using one of the non-targeted sequences

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.000
or one that we don't have a separate database for.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:24.000
Notice that I can pick right now the 16S ribosomal RNA sequences that we have as a reference collection.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.000
And those are the ones that I would want to use to try to identify these unknown organisms

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.000
or partially-identified organisms that are in this set.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.000
So I can pick that as my database.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.000
And then notice like on the regular BLAST page, there are some advanced parameters here.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.000
I can choose my Search program.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.000
I'm going to use Megablast; it's faster.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.000
It picks a set of sequences for me.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.000
It can also exclude things.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
If I'm going to search against the NR database, it might be useful to exclude uncultured sample sequences

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.000
because they won't give me much in the way of confirming my identification.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Also notice that I can check this "Sequences from type material" box, which is very useful.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.000
When we search our 16S rRNA database,

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.000
what you're going to find is that many of those are going to be from type material.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:16.000
And that would give you some confirmation about the identity of the organism involved.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:22.000
MOLE-BLAST will cluster query sequences by loci -- what those loci often are,

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.000
are going to be different genes if you have a mixed set.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.000
In many cases, the cluster is going to be governed by how good your sequences are, how long they are,

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.000
and what the quality of the alignments are as well.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.000
We can adjust those parameters.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.000
For example, this example that I'm going to do right now, you could go back and you can play around with this

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:46.000
and see if you can change the number of clusters by changing the percent identity.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:52.000
One thing that's very helpful to do with MOLE-BLAST because we don't have an Edit and Resubmit

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.000
is to hit the "Show sequence results in new window" and rerun it.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.000
I'm going to go ahead and just submit that.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.000
But because MOLE-BLAST takes a couple of minutes to run, I'm just going to go ahead

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:08.000
and get the results because I have them stored.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:16.000
So I'm going to go back here to my Submission Page and hit the Recent Results tab here.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:21.000
Notice that MOLE-BLAST stores RIDs exactly the same way that BLAST does.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:33.000
So I'm just going to retrieve one; I've got them saved here in a little text pad so I can remember what they are.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.000
I'm just going to copy that one.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:40.000
[Copying]

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.000
And retrieve my results.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:49.000
And so the default display is to show you a tree based on that multiple alignment.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.000
This one would be very useful if I was interested in knowing, for example,

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.000
the kinds of taxonomic units that were in my environmental sample.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:03.000
And I can see on the right-hand side, they are color coded here by major groups of bacteria or the classes of bacteria.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:13.000
From this page, I can also get to the tree itself and download it in some formats that I can work with locally.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.000
I can also see an alignment.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:24.000
If I click here, I can get to the MUSCLE based Multiple Sequence Alignment; so there it is.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.000
This is a large number of sequences.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.000
We had 50+, the database sequences that I'd asked for.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:39.000
Many of these NR sequences here are going to be our reference collection of 16S ribosomal RNA sequences.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.000
I notice from this page I can also edit and resubmit this to run MUSCLE again. [Since this webinar, the Re-align option was removed]

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:49.000
If I decided there were some sequences that I don't want to include, I can remove them

00:07:49.000 --> 00:08:01.000
and realign the whole thing by going up here to the top and just realign them.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.000
Let me go back to the tree view here.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:09.000
If you're familiar with our tree view, all the same functionality exists here that exists on the BLAST pages.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:20.000
I can display smaller sections of the tree, and I can splice smaller sections of the multiple sequence alignment.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.000
I think that's all we really have time for today.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:28.000
I just wanted to make sure that everybody knew about MOLE-BLAST and how to get to it and what you can do with it.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:35.000
There are some additional examples that are available on that file on the FTP site.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:40.000
And so that's the conclusion basically of one of these NCBI Minutes.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.000
They're intended to be fairly short.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:48.000
We'll have another one next week on the 15th.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.000
Like I said, we don't have time to answer questions orally; but if you put them in the Questions pod,

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:58.000
we will try to get them and get them up on a document on the Webinars Page.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.000
Thank you very much for coming to the first NCBI Minute.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:03.000
Make sure that you check out our YouTube channel

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:07.000
and subscribe to that so you can see whenever we put new videos up there.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:13.620
They're not going to just be the webinar videos.
There will also be individual recordings of how to do things and other types of things like that.

